  "articleBody": "It's easy to make a few slip-ups when it comes to finish carpentry, especially if you're not a seasoned pro. One of the biggest mistakes is poor communication about the project's scope. You really need to be clear about what you envision, so talk through every detail with your contractor before they even pick up a tool. Another common issue is choosing someone without specific experience in finish work. This isn't just about general carpentry; finish carpentry requires a different level of precision and attention to detail, especially for things like custom trim or cabinetry. You don't want someone trying to learn on your dime. And, oh boy, the classic mistake of relying on caulk or paint to hide imperfect joints! A good finish carpenter gets the joints right from the start; caulk is for sealing, not for fixing poor craftsmanship. Instead of hoping for the best, make sure your contractor has a solid portfolio and references from similar projects. When you've got tight reveal lines, custom pieces, or challenging installations like stair parts, that's when a truly qualified professional becomes essential. At HH Remodeling LLC, we focus on getting those details perfect, preventing those common headaches.",
